# Migrating `TimedExecutor` to .NET 8

## Key Problem

`Thread.Abort()` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` throw `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 8. The cooperative cancellation pattern using `CancellationToken` is the modern replacement.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Will throw if the task doesn't complete in time
            task.Wait(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }

        // Unwrap AggregateException for single faults
        if (task.IsFaulted)
            throw task.Exception!.InnerExceptions.Count == 1
                ? task.Exception.InnerException!
                : task.Exception;

        return task.Result;
    }

    // Async overload (preferred in modern code)
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await work(cts.Token).WaitAsync(timeout);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

## Usage

```csharp
var executor = new TimedExecutor();

// The work delegate must observe the token
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(ct =>
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++)
    {
        ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // cooperative check
        // ... do work ...
    }
    return 42;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
```

## Migration Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **Non-cooperative work won't stop** | Unlike `Thread.Abort()`, cancellation is cooperative. If `work` never checks the token (e.g., blocking on unmanaged I/O), it keeps running after timeout. |
| **Fire-and-forget leak** | After timeout, the abandoned `Task` still holds resources. For CPU-bound work, always check `ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` in loops. |
| **`Thread.ResetAbort()` removal** | No equivalent needed—there's no abort to reset. Remove all `ThreadAbortException` handling. |
| **Exception semantics change** | `Task.Run` wraps in `AggregateException`; use `.GetAwaiter().GetResult()` or unwrap manually if callers expect the raw exception. |
| **`default(T)` race condition** | The original code had a race: if `thread.Join` returned `true` but `result` wasn't yet visible due to memory model quirks. `Task<T>` handles this correctly. |

## For truly uncooperative code

If you **must** kill unresponsive work (e.g., third-party library with no cancellation support), isolate it in a separate **process** rather than a thread:

```csharp
// Last resort: out-of-process execution
var process = Process.Start(new ProcessStartInfo { /* ... */ });
if (!process.WaitForExit(timeout))
{
    process.Kill(entireProcessTree: true);
    throw new TimeoutException("...");
}
```

There is no safe in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort()` in .NET 8.
